sing the method for length problem, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a length problem problem with numbers such as 3 and 150, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Adding 3 + 150 without converting units invents a nonsense length","Use the same length problem method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 3 and 150 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers"],"question":"Solve a fresh length problem problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Label units when comparing 3 with 150","Adding 3 + 150 without converting units invents a nonsense length","Length problems combine measures using addition, subtraction or comparison while keeping units consistent","3 m + 1 m 50 cm = 4 m 50 cm (convert 150 cm first)"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for length problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for length problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses length problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for length problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about length problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with length problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Adding 3 + 150 without converting units invents a nonsense length.. Correction uses 3 m + 1 m 50 cm = 4 m 50 cm (convert 150 cm first).. Why: restores accurate length problem.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w22-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise length problem.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence.