RKED MODEL\nA ribbon 120 cm and another 85 cm total 205 cm = 2 m 5 cm. Difference 120−85=35 cm.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA path is 3 m long and extended by 150 cm. New length in metres and centimetres?\n\nFEEDBACK\n3 m + 1 m 50 cm = 4 m 50 cm (convert 150 cm first).","explain":"Length problems combine measures using addition, subtraction or comparison while keeping units consistent.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Adding 3 + 150 without converting units invents a nonsense length. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Length total with unit conversion.","caption":"Convert before adding different unit forms.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Total</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">120 cm + 85 cm</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Result</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">205 cm = 2 m 5 cm</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A ribbon 120 cm and another 85 cm total 205 cm = 2 m 5 cm. Difference 120−85=35 cm. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A ribbon 120 cm and another 85 cm total 205 cm = 2 m 5 cm. Difference 120−85=35 cm. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about length problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA ribbon 120 cm and another 85 cm total 205 cm = 2 m 5 cm. Difference 120−85=35 cm. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and 